ine  owned by rwe the biggest energy company  in germany  the backbone of this nation's industrial  might  imarat was flattened to allow for the  expansion of this  massive open cast coal mine which  supplies a local power station  that is one of the most polluting in the  whole of europe  now the german government has got a plan  to phase out coal but at the same time  this coal mine has got plans to get even  bigger and that would mean the  demolition of more local villages  this is one of them lutzerrad most  residents have sold up and moved  but protesters have come in their place  determined to halt the destruction  and some of them living in trees as they  plan how to stop the looming demolition  we are rich in germany we have  a high income high living standards and  we need to  start making changes here because we can  so why do it rwe told us that abandoning  coal power  has to happen gradually it's got  something to do with the law  and this coal exit law says that um  guards viola mine is necessary  for the security of supply in germany  beyond 2030. nobody here  claims that coal is clean but they do  say it's vital  for the moment adam parsons sky news  western germany  while activists like those are hoping to  stop further expansion  of the godzvila coal mine in their  country one german environmentalist is  taking a more global approach  23 year old felix finkbiner founded  plant for the planet  to unite children and young people in a  mission to bring back  a trillion trees around the world here's  his story  i'm felix finkbeiner of plant for the  planet a children and youth organization  with the mission of  reforesting the planet to fight the  climate crisis  for us it all started about 14 years ago  when i was  nine years old and in fourth grade for  us children  forests are our future i was supposed to  give a little presentation in my class  about the climate crisis  and when i prepared that presentation i  found out about vangari mathai  [Music]  and that inspired us that we kids could  also be planting trees to fight the  climate crisis  so i told my classmates about it they  liked the idea and that's why a few  weeks later  we planted the first tree after one year  we had planted about 50  000 trees after three years the first  million and more and more children  started joining us all across the world  [Music]  on top of just calling on the world and  calling on others to restore forests we  of course want to plant as many trees as  possible  ourselves and this is why on the yucatan  peninsula in southern mexico  we have a wonderful team of just over  100 people doing their best  to restore and reforest um degraded and  destroyed forests  and we managed to plant up to two  million trees per year so far  and we hope to plant three million trees  this year alone  [Applause]  well in today's other climate news  police scotland  say the policing of protests at the cop  26 conference in glasgow will be  welcoming and friendly there are plans  to have 10  000 officers deployed each day during  the event that's due to take place in  november  it comes after the government announced  it will offer coronavirus vaccines to  cop  26 delegates who are otherwise unable to  access them  lloyd's of london the world's biggest  insurance market is  teaming up with prince charles to  support the environment  together they are leading the  sustainable markets initiative insurance  task force  it's made up of bosses from 17 of the  uk's largest  insurers it aims to support the  transition to a less  carbon-intensive economy part of our  challenge is to turn around and say how  do we partner with governments to say  actually there are insurance products  and there's a risk management service  proposition  that we could partner with the developed  economies to help support emerging  economies  through those disaster scenarios so  that's one of the ideas that we think we  can explore with government  smart way of putting money at the front  of a disaster rather than putting money  behind a disaster so let's be  preemptive and proactive rather than  reactive post loss  and scientists in greece are  investigating whether a layer of cease  not  in the aegean sea is connected to a  similar outbreak that's threatening  marine life in turkey the environment  ministry is using satellites to collect  data  after authorities on lemnos reported  seeing  mucus spots on parts of the island's  coastline  scientists are warning the so-called  c-snot is increasing  due to climate change and water  pollution  now there are calls for urgent action  for more to be done  to protect puffins the endangered birds  are native to the uk  but overfishing by scandinavian boats  off the coast of scotland  as well as the effects of climate change  are putting them at  even greater risk as skies james  matthews reports  [Music]  it's a hard life being a puffin and it's  getting harder  to look in their beak is to look at the  problem the sand eels the puffin fishes  to feed its young  are becoming smaller and harder to find  climate change is a factor  warming of the seas the plankton moving  north the sand deals following  and it's a real concern you know that  just slight increase in the in the sea  temperature  is having a big effect on the seabirds  which nests behind me here  overfishing of the sand deal in places  like this the isle of may  off scotland's east coast is also an  issue  combine that with climate change and for  the puffin  it's a perfect storm puffins need  enough food during the breeding season  to feed the chicks  that's the kind of cycle of life that  they need if they're not enough food at  the right time  then they're not able to successfully  breed and raise chicks and then  populations  populations go down so what we're seeing  is is  warming waters changing the composition  of the zooplankton  which is having a knock-on effect right  through the food chain into san diego so  there isn't the available sand deals at  the right time for puffins to feed their  chicks  there is a puffin chip it is a concern  for a sea bird  much loved and much hampered by changing  circumstances  the puffin is already deemed at risk of  global extinction  a popular symbol of nature's emergency  needing address james matthews sky news  on the isle of may  prince william has announced that london  will host the first awards ceremony  for his multi-million pound earthshot  prize  the nobel style environmental scheme  aims to discover and to scale  the best solutions to help repair our  planet  and tackle the climate crisis well we  can bring in eric levine now from  countess inn who are working with  earthshot prize and are encouraging  everyone  to take small steps to reduce their  carbon impact  eric good to talk to you a million  pounds is quite the incentive isn't it  you'd be tempted to have a go  do you think it will come up with  implementable solutions  i think it's yes and samantha wonderful  to be with you and and counter sin is  really proud partner of the earthshot  prize and and so looking forward to the  17th of october alexander palace  implementable i definitely think this  invitation  is going to bring forward some um some  breakthrough ideas and we need all of  them but the earthshot prize is  it's more than a prize it's always been  more than a prize it's about inviting  the best that our innovators  uh can bring to the table and things  that could really be game changing and  it's also about mobilizing  all of us uh to find the ear shots in  our own lives and and what can i do in  my  in my family this week that's an earth  shot in my own life or in my in my  workplace with my colleagues next week  uh and and all of us doing that together  yeah that's exactly the point isn't it  those encouraged to enter  not just big business or governments but  any individual  young or old and those solutions could  be big or small  yeah yeah that's absolutely right we  really do need uh  our our governments and our and our  cities to shift and to be  ambitious and we need bit we need  businesses to look at their own  transformation  engage their colleagues and engage their  customers but it's really a question  this this decisive decade  2020 to 2030 where the prize is  is is giving out um you know these these  five  opportunities for a breakthrough in a  spotlight but it's really a clarion call  for all of us  um to rally behind and that's why doing  it together  uh it is it is possible if we act  quickly and if we act  ambitiously doing any of it alone um  uh won't cut the mustard no one prize no  one business no one government  can provide the solution but all of us  together can add up to something bigger  and that's why the prize and count us in  have invited people to do that and be  counted together and then the platform  that we have where you can go and take  practical steps  in your own life use your voice reduce  your carbon footprint  and it can and you can see the impact of  that and have it all add up together  well we look forward to hearing about  those prize-winning submissions eric  levine thank you for talking to us  wonderful to be with you well that's  everything from us for this week but  don't forget to check out our digital  show  over the weekend in the latest episode  we go behind the scenes of inzamam  rashid's investigation  into the uk's acid tar lagoons that's  available  for you on sky news social channels our  app  and website and on climatecast  this week we dig deeper into the causes  and effects of  air pollution and look at how it can  amplify  not only the impact of covid but social  injustice as well  that is available wherever you normally  get your podcast from  anna jones will be back next week at our  normal time  here on sky news thanks for watching

